Z||000003||mrfiero||08-11-2001||12:40 AM||||The '88 upper balljoints are tough to find, but most any parts store will carry the lowers.  $39 is a good price, but what brand is it?  I prefer Moog, but they are a lot more expensive (about $55-$60 each).<P>As for changing them.....remove the cotter pin and loosen the castle nut to the lower balljoint (don't remove the nut).  Now tap the spindle, where the stud goes though, with a hammer to loosen things up and whack the top of the stud/castle nut with the hammer to pop it loose from the spindle.<P>The control arm won't go anywhere......the shock and sway bar end link will support the control arm.  Remove the nut and lift off the spindle from the control arm. <P>You should have enough room (although you may need to turn the steering wheel all the way to one side) to whack the balljoint out of the control arm with a sledgehammer (BFH, for short   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> )<P>Rent/borrow a balljoint installation tool (Auto Zone carries them) and press in the new joint.<P>I usually remove the entire control arm, but you don't necessarily have to do this.  <P>If you do remove the entire arm you might want to replace the bushings too ($15 apiece for stock rubber).<P>It's a fairly easy job to do.....I have replaced lower balljoints on 6 or 7 Fieros and have it down to a science (takes me about an hour to do both).<P>Good luck and don't forget an alignment! <p>[This message has been edited by mrfiero (edited 08-11-2001).]||||reg||6
Z||000004||montage||08-11-2001||11:00 AM||||Check the brand of ball joint, Moog seams to be the best choice as far as I can determine. Moog ball joints cost here between $65-70 but Auto Value's brand name Partsmaster are made by Moog and I can get those for $35. Ask who makes your Auto Parts store brand name.<P>As for installation I highly recomend a ball joint press. It is so easy with one, you can rent (some places rent for free) them or buy them for ~$30 for cheap ones. I knew a guy who had torches, BFH, and beat on the ball joint for a good 15 min. after heating it up. Finally, I talked him into using my ball joint press and it took less than 2 min. to take the ball joint out and you don't take the chance of beating the piss out of the control arm.||||reg||1
